---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleCard from "../../components/ArticleCard.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles"))
  .filter((article) => article.data.category === "Food Shortages")
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Food Shortages | Food Supply Crisis Report"
  description="Evidence-based reporting on localized food shortages, supply disruptions, availability risks and how to distinguish shortages from higher prices or temporary pressure."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Topic Hub</p>
      <h1>Food Shortages</h1>
      <p>Understand where shortages are occurring, what is causing them, and how localized availability problems differ from broad food-price or global supply trends.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2>How we distinguish a shortage from a warning</h2>
      <p>A shortage claim should state which food is affected, where it is affected, when the problem occurred and whether it is temporary or persistent. A photograph of an empty shelf can document an individual moment, but it cannot establish the scale or cause of a wider supply problem by itself.</p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>Local stock-out</h3><p>Check the specific product, store or area and whether normal deliveries restore availability.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Regional distribution problem</h3><p>Look for evidence across multiple markets, transport routes, wholesalers or local authority notices.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>National supply stress</h3><p>Compare production, stocks, imports, exports, policy changes and price data from current official sources.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Acute food insecurity</h3><p>Use humanitarian assessments that identify affected populations and severity rather than assuming it from retail availability alone.</p></article>
      </div>
      <h3>A simple verification sequence</h3>
      <p>First find the original source and its publication date. Next verify the place and the commodity. Then seek a second independent indicator: market data for a stock-out, crop and stock data for a supply claim, or IPC/WFP evidence for acute food insecurity. Keep forecasts labelled as forecasts. Our <a href="/articles/food-collapse-fact-check-2026/">food-collapse fact check</a> shows how to apply this test to common viral claims.</p>
      <p>We do not treat preparedness guidance, price inflation and famine risk as interchangeable. Each can be important, but the evidence and the appropriate response differ.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      {articles.length ? (
        <div class="card-grid">
          {articles.map((article) => <ArticleCard article={article} />)}
        </div>
      ) : (
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Food-shortage coverage:</strong> New reporting will be added with source links and clear distinction between localized shortages, temporary disruptions and wider supply constraints.
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
